#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read and write the `shipmate / gate` commit status: the one script that does either. Its
`checks` subcommand serves `_shipmate.check_runs` to the bash sites.

    gate-status newest <sha>                     print `<state> <run id>` of the newest gate
    gate-status refuse-newer <sha>               exit `NEWER` when a newer run holds the gate
    gate-status post <sha> <state> <description> write the gate
    gate-status hold <sha> <description>         write a `failure` gate
    gate-status publishing <sha>                 write this run's `pending` publish hold
    gate-status end <sha> <description>          turn this run's publish hold `failure`
    gate-status refresh <sha>                    green the gate once every apply check is done
    gate-status deploy <sha> <detect> <results>  write the post-merge verdict over job results
    gate-status checks <sha> <jq>                print one `jq` projection per line of check runs
    gate-status moved <sha> <pr>                 print `<pr>`'s live head when it is not `<sha>`
    gate-status replan <sha> <pr>                print that live head when it needs a plan
    gate-status published <sha> <pr>             print the earlier run that published it on `<pr>`

The gate is a commit status, not a check-run: a check-run binds to a check-suite and can land in
a stale one when a commit carries two plan runs, which blocks the merge forever. Every write sets
`target_url` to this run. Env: `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`, `GITHUB_SERVER_URL`,
`GITHUB_RUN_ID`, `SHIPMATE_APP_ID` for `refresh`, and `SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG`, the shipmate App's
slug, for `newest`, `refuse-newer`, `refresh`, `post`, `end` and `published`: each refuses an
empty one before any API call, and looks up the id of the App's bot user `<slug>[bot]` once,
before its first statuses listing; an invocation that lists no statuses makes no lookup. A
`<sha>` that is not 40 lowercase hex characters is refused before any API call. A failed API
call exits 1 with an `::error::` line, except in `checks`, `moved`, `replan` and `published`. A
call that fails transiently retries twice, each retry noted on stderr. A gate write (`post`, `hold`,
`publishing`, `end`, `refresh`, `deploy`) that GitHub's secondary rate limit refuses also waits
60 s and retries, noted on stderr, up to three waits per invocation.
A hold (`is_hold`) is a `failure` gate or a publish hold: a `pending` gate whose description is
`PUBLISH_HOLD` naming the run of its `target_url`, which a plan run writes while it publishes.
`end` turns this run's publish hold `failure` with its description; when the newest gate is
anything else it prints why, writes nothing and exits 0.
`refresh` over a hold prints why, unannotated, and exits `HELD`, also when the hold appears while
its write retries: each caller decides whether a hold fails its step. A hold from this
run or an older one is read again first, up to three times two seconds apart, since a statuses
read right after this run's own write can return the write before it; only a status write
newer than the hold, by status id, clears it. `refuse-newer` prints its
refusal as an `::error::` line and exits `NEWER`, so a caller tells a newer run from a failed
read (exit 1); `post` re-runs it before each retry, so it can exit `NEWER` too. `checks`
serves `_shipmate.check_runs` to the bash sites: UTF-8 with LF line ends
on any host, and a malformed `<sha>` or a failed listing exits 1 unannotated, a failed listing
with the command and gh's stderr after any retry notices: a caller that degrades prints its own
warning, and a fail-closed caller prints its own `::error::`.

`moved` and `replan` read the pull request through `_shipmate.pr_head`. `replan` prints the
live head when it is not `<sha>`, the pull request is open and not a draft, and no run of the
live head planned or is about to plan it; otherwise it prints nothing, with one log line on
stderr when the head moved. It reads, stopping at the first run that blocks:

1. the live head's autoplan runs (`apply-gate.autoplan_runs`): one not `completed` blocks;
2. the live `shipmate plan` runs of `<pr>`, any actor (`_shipmate.live_plan_runs`), other than
   this run (`GITHUB_RUN_ID`): one blocks;
3. the jobs of each autoplan run `completed` with conclusion `success`: one that planned
   (`apply-gate.planned`) blocks. Jobs that carry another run attempt than the listing (a re-run
   between the two reads) are read again on the next listing and, on the last, cannot judge.
   Any other completed run blocks nothing: a draft-skipped or deferred run, a `closed`-event
   run, a cancelled or failed one.
4. When nothing blocks, the autoplan runs are listed again every `REPLAN_POLL` seconds up to
   `REPLAN_WAIT`, since the push that moved the head can take seconds to list its autoplan.
   Each listing is judged whole as in 1 and 3: a re-run keeps its run id and can turn queued.
   A run attempt's jobs are read once.
5. After the wait, the pull request is read again: a head that moved again, a closed pull
   request or a draft dispatches nothing. Then the live plan runs are read once more.

Step 2 relies on the consumer's `plan-<pr>` concurrency group, which runs one plan run of the
pull request at a time: while this run holds it, a listed live plan run has not yet read its
head, so it plans the live head.

A `<pr>` that is not a pull-request number exits 1 with `::error::` before any API call. A
malformed `<sha>`, a failed head read, a failed or malformed listing, an autoplan run without a
str `status` (and a str `conclusion` once `completed`) or an int `run_attempt`, an incomplete
jobs listing and a `plan / detect` job `planned` cannot judge exit 1 with one unannotated
stderr line, after any retry notices and warnings: an unreadable answer dispatches nothing.

`published` prints the earlier autoplan run of `<sha>` that planned and published it on `<pr>`
by the rules in `_published`, or nothing. Its `<pr>` and `<sha>` are refused as `replan`'s, and
every failed or malformed read exits 1 with one unannotated stderr line: the caller plans.
"""

import functools
import json
import os
import re
import sys
from urllib.parse import quote

from _shipmate import (
    _CREATED_AT,
    GATE_CONTEXT,
    _load,
    _run,
    attempt_jobs,
    check_runs,
    current_run_url,
    exit_text,
    gh_json,
    is_pr_number,
    is_run_id,
    one_line,
    other_live_plan_run,
    pr_head,
    retried,
    run,
    run_annotated,
    sleep,
    status_text,
    validate_head_sha,
    write_retried,
)

ag = _load("apply-gate")

_RUN_ID = re.compile(r"/actions/runs/([0-9]+)$")
#: The fields `apply-gate` judges.
_CHECK_RUNS_JQ = ".check_runs[] | {name, status, conclusion, started_at, id, app: {id: .app.id}}"
_STATUSES_JQ = ".[] | {context, state, description, target_url, id, creator: .creator.id}"
#: The publish hold's description: `publishing` writes it and `is_hold` reads it, so the two
#: cannot drift.
PUBLISH_HOLD = (
    "Plan run {run} holds this while it publishes; if that run has ended, re-plan (comment "
    "shipmate plan or push)"
)
_GOOD_RESULTS = {"success", "skipped"}
HELD = 3
NEWER = 4
#: How long `replan` waits for the push that moved the head to list its autoplan run: a push, a
#: ready click and a reopen each listed theirs within 7.8 s of the pull request showing it, over
#: five trials each on a sample repository in November 2026.
REPLAN_WAIT = 15
REPLAN_POLL = 5
#: How often, and how many seconds apart, `refresh` reads again a hold this run or an older
#: one wrote: a statuses read right after a write returned the write before it in 8 of 40
#: probes, and in 5 of 30 in a second run, where each lag was one read and converged within 0.97 s.
HOLD_REREADS = 3
HOLD_REREAD_WAIT = 2
_COMMENTS_JQ = '.[] | {login: .user.login, lines: ((.body // "") | split("\\n")[0:4])}'


def _repo():
    return os.environ["GITHUB_REPOSITORY"]


def deploy_verdict(detect, results):
    """(state, description) over a deploy run's `detect` result and its comma-separated job
    results.

    An empty `detect` is a caller that passed no result. A `detect` that did not succeed applied
    nothing: it refused or stopped after writing the head.
    Otherwise any result but `success` or `skipped` (failure, cancelled, timed_out) is incomplete,
    and so is an empty `results`: no env-level job reported, so nothing was applied."""
    if not detect:
        return "failure", "deploy gate got no detect result; see the run"
    if detect != "success":
        return "failure", f"deploy stopped at detect ({detect}), nothing applied; see the run"
    tokens = results.split(",")
    if all(t in _GOOD_RESULTS for t in tokens):
        return "success", "all env-levels applied (deploy env-level applies completed)"
    return "failure", "deploy incomplete (deploy env-level applies completed)"


def _require_slug():
    """`SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG`, or an `::error::` exit when it is empty or unset."""
    slug = os.environ.get("SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG", "")
    if not slug:
        raise SystemExit(
            "::error::SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG is empty; gate-status reads only the gate statuses the "
            "shipmate App created and cannot tell which those are"
        )
    return slug


@functools.cache
def app_user():
    """The integer id of the shipmate App's bot user, `<SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG>[bot]`, read once per
    process. A failed read (after `gh_json`'s retries), an answer that is not JSON, or an `id`
    that is not an integer exits 1 with one `::error::` line naming the bot user."""
    login = f"{_require_slug()}[bot]"
    lead = f"::error::could not read the shipmate App's bot user {login}"
    try:
        user = gh_json(f"users/{quote(login, safe='')}", _run)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise SystemExit(f"{lead}: {one_line(str(exc.code).removeprefix('::error::'))}") from None
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(f"{lead}: {one_line(exc)}") from None
    uid = user.get("id") if isinstance(user, dict) else None
    if type(uid) is not int:
        raise SystemExit(f"{lead}: its id is {json.dumps(uid)}")
    return uid


def _gates(sha):
    """`(state, run id, status id, description)` of every gate status on `sha` the shipmate App
    created, newest first.

    A status's creator must be the App's bot user (`app_user`), read before the listing: any
    token with `statuses: write` can post this context, so a status from another identity would
    otherwise add or hide a hold. That identity is never defaulted: without it no status counts.
    The run id is the `/actions/runs/<id>` suffix of `target_url`, `-` when it has none; a missing
    or null description is `""`, which is never a publish hold. The
    status id is the one GitHub assigns each write, increasing: it orders two writes of one run,
    as a re-run attempt's, where the run id cannot. The statuses listing is paginated: a head
    with many other contexts can push its gate past the first page."""
    app = app_user()
    args = [
        "gh",
        "api",
        "--paginate",
        f"repos/{_repo()}/commits/{sha}/statuses?per_page=100",
        "--jq",
        _STATUSES_JQ,
    ]
    out = retried(lambda: _run(args))
    gates = []
    for status in ag.parse_jsonl(out.splitlines()):
        if status.get("context") == GATE_CONTEXT and status["creator"] == app:
            m = _RUN_ID.search(status.get("target_url") or "")
            run_id = m.group(1) if m else "-"
            gates.append((status["state"], run_id, status["id"], status.get("description") or ""))
    return gates


def newest_gate(sha):
    """(state, run id, status id, description) of the newest gate status the shipmate App created
    on `sha`, or None when it carries none. Statuses from other identities are not counted."""
    gates = _gates(sha)
    return gates[0] if gates else None


def _publish_hold(gate):
    """Whether `gate`, a `_gates` tuple, is the publish hold of the run its `target_url` names:
    state and whole description, since that run's final gate can be `pending` too."""
    state, run_id, _, description = gate
    return state == "pending" and description == PUBLISH_HOLD.format(run=run_id)


def is_hold(gate):
    """Whether `gate`, a `_gates` tuple, holds the gate: a `failure` or a publish hold. No
    refresh clears either; a new plan run replaces both, `end` turns this run's publish hold
    `failure`, and a merge deploy's verdict covers any hold on the merged head."""
    return gate[0] == "failure" or _publish_hold(gate)


def newer_hold(sha):
    """The highest run id among the holds (`is_hold`) on `sha` written by a run newer than this
    one, or None.

    Every App-created status in the history counts, not only the newest: this run's own hold,
    written after a failed read, must not hide a newer run's earlier one, nor that run's final
    gate its own publish hold. Only plan runs write a hold before a merge, so a newer one is a
    newer plan of the same head."""
    this_run = _this_run()
    runs = [
        int(gate[1])
        for gate in _gates(sha)
        if is_hold(gate) and gate[1] != "-" and int(gate[1]) > this_run
    ]
    return max(runs) if runs else None


def _this_run():
    this_run = os.environ.get("GITHUB_RUN_ID", "")
    if not is_run_id(this_run):
        raise SystemExit(f"::error::GITHUB_RUN_ID must be a decimal run id (got: {this_run!r})")
    return int(this_run)


def _may_lag(gate):
    """Whether `gate` is a hold (`is_hold`) from this run or an older one: a statuses read that
    lags this run's own write returns one. A newer run's hold, or one with run id `-`, is final."""
    if gate is None or not is_hold(gate) or gate[1] == "-":
        return False
    return int(gate[1]) <= _this_run()


def _refuse_held(sha):
    """Exit `HELD` when the newest gate on `sha` is a hold (`is_hold`), reading again up to
    `HOLD_REREADS` times while that hold may lag this run's own write (`_may_lag`).

    A re-read showing a gate that is no hold clears the hold only when its status id is higher
    than that of every hold read so far: any other is a stale read under a hold. A re-read
    showing a hold that cannot lag refuses at once. Accepted cost: a hold an apply or deploy run
    refreshes over waits `HOLD_REREADS * HOLD_REREAD_WAIT` seconds before it refuses."""
    newest = newest_gate(sha)
    hold = newest if newest and is_hold(newest) else None
    highest = hold[2] if hold else 0
    for _ in range(HOLD_REREADS):
        if hold is None or not _may_lag(hold):
            break
        sleep(HOLD_REREAD_WAIT)
        newest = newest_gate(sha)
        if newest and is_hold(newest):
            highest = max(highest, newest[2])
            if not _may_lag(newest):
                hold = newest
        elif newest and newest[2] > highest:
            hold = None
    if hold:
        print(
            f"{GATE_CONTEXT} is held for {sha}; its status description on the pull request names "
            "the cause. A hold clears only when a new plan run rewrites the gate: comment "
            "shipmate plan, or push."
        )
        raise SystemExit(HELD)


def post(sha, state, description, before_retry=None):
    """Write the gate through `write_retried`, `before_retry` before each retry. A repeated POST
    of a status that landed only adds an identical history entry.

    Known ceilings: a retry re-posts over a hold its `before_retry` does not see, as a first POST
    already can over one written just before it (`gate-hold` writes outside the gate group). The
    `post` command re-runs `refuse-newer` and `refresh` its own hold check; `hold` and `deploy`
    re-check nothing, nor do `publishing` and `end`. `hold`, `publishing` and `end` write a
    hold, so their re-posts green nothing. A `deploy` write,
    first POST or retry, covers any hold written on the merged head before it, whether a plan run
    wrote it in the gate group (a run admitted before the merge can write one after it) or
    `gate-hold` wrote it outside the group. The merged head's gate then gates no merge unless
    another open pull request shares that head.

    Newlines in `description` become spaces: a status description is one line, and `is_transient`
    reads every line after the argv as gh's stderr."""
    args = [
        "gh",
        "api",
        f"repos/{_repo()}/statuses/{sha}",
        "-f",
        f"state={state}",
        "-f",
        f"context={GATE_CONTEXT}",
        "-f",
        f"description={' '.join(description.split())[:140]}",
        "-f",
        f"target_url={current_run_url()}",
    ]
    write_retried(lambda: _run(args), before_retry=before_retry)


def refresh(sha):
    """Green the gate when every App-authored apply check on `sha` is done and no hold
    (`is_hold`) stands: an absent gate or a final `pending` one greens.

    A hold must not be cleared by the applies it exists to prevent: `shipmate apply` never
    consults the gate, so the parsed cells can all complete while stacks whose summaries were
    never read have no apply check at all. Only a fresh plan run exits a hold.

    A check re-created mid-apply stays open until its own plan is applied: `apply-gate` judges
    the newest run per name."""
    _require_slug()
    out = check_runs(_repo(), sha, _CHECK_RUNS_JQ)
    runs = ag.app_runs(out.splitlines(), os.environ.get("SHIPMATE_APP_ID", ""))
    if ag.verdict(runs) == "pending":
        open_ = sorted(set(ag.latest_by_name(runs)) - ag.done_names(runs))
        print(
            f"apply checks on {sha} are not all done ({len(open_)} open); the gate is left as it is"
        )
        return
    _refuse_held(sha)
    post(
        sha,
        "success",
        "all applies complete, nothing left to apply",
        before_retry=lambda: _refuse_held(sha),
    )


def _newest(sha):
    newest = newest_gate(sha)
    if newest:
        print(*newest[:2])


def _refuse_newer(sha):
    newer = newer_hold(sha)
    if newer is not None:
        print(
            f"::error::plan run {newer} holds the gate of {sha} and is newer than this run "
            f"({os.environ['GITHUB_RUN_ID']}); this run stops without writing its final gate. "
            "Re-plan this pull request (comment shipmate plan or push) if that run did not "
            "finish.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        raise SystemExit(NEWER)


def _end(sha, description):
    """Write `failure` with `description` over this run's own publish hold. Any other newest gate
    stays: a newer run's gate, this run's final gate, or a gate this run did not write."""
    newest, own = newest_gate(sha), str(_this_run())
    if newest is None or newest[1] != own or not _publish_hold(newest):
        shown = f"{newest[0]} from run {newest[1]}" if newest else "none"
        print(
            f"the newest gate of {sha} is not plan run {own}'s publish hold (newest: {shown}); "
            "the gate is left as it is"
        )
        return
    post(sha, "failure", description)


def _post(sha, state, description):
    _require_slug()
    # The summary writes after `refuse-newer`; a retried write re-runs it first.
    post(sha, state, description, lambda: _refuse_newer(sha))


def _checks(sha, jq):
    # Bytes, not `print`: a Windows text stdout re-encodes through the code page and writes CRLF.
    sys.stdout.buffer.write(check_runs(_repo(), sha, jq, run=run).encode("utf-8"))


def _moved(sha, pr):
    live, _, _ = pr_head(_repo(), pr, run=run)
    if live != sha:
        print(live)


def _autoplans(live):
    """`live`'s autoplan runs, ascending by id, each with a str `status`, a str `conclusion` once
    `completed`, and an int `run_attempt`."""
    try:
        runs = ag.autoplan_runs(_repo(), live, run=run)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(exit_text(exc))) from None
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(exc)) from None
    for r in runs:
        try:
            ag.status_and_conclusion(r, f"autoplan run {r['id']}")
        except ValueError as exc:
            raise SystemExit(one_line(exc)) from None
        if type(r.get("run_attempt")) is not int:
            raise SystemExit(
                one_line(f"autoplan run {r['id']} has run attempt {r.get('run_attempt')!r}")
            )
    return sorted(runs, key=lambda r: r["id"])


def _unfinished(runs):
    for r in runs:
        if r["status"] != "completed":
            return f", which has an autoplan run {r['id']} in progress"
    return None


def _jobs(run_id):
    try:
        return attempt_jobs(_repo(), run_id, f"autoplan run {run_id}", run)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(str(exc)) from None


def _planned(run_id, jobs):
    try:
        return ag.planned(jobs)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(f"autoplan run {run_id}: {exc}")) from None


def _succeeded(runs, evidence, last):
    """The block reason of the first run of `runs` that succeeded and planned, or None. Jobs are
    read once per run attempt into `evidence`, and judged and kept only when every job carries
    the listed attempt; otherwise they are read again on the next listing, and on the `last`
    one, when no run blocks, the run cannot be judged."""
    mismatch = None
    for r in runs:
        if r["conclusion"] != "success":
            continue
        key = (r["id"], r["run_attempt"])
        if key not in evidence:
            jobs, attempts = _jobs(r["id"])
            if attempts != [r["run_attempt"]]:
                mismatch = mismatch or (
                    f"autoplan run {r['id']} is listed at run attempt {r['run_attempt']} and "
                    f"its jobs at {attempts}"
                )
                continue
            evidence[key] = _planned(r["id"], jobs)
        if evidence[key]:
            return f", whose autoplan run {r['id']} succeeded"
    if mismatch and last:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(mismatch))
    return None


def _stop(pr, live, draft, state):
    """Why a pull request in `state` and `draft` wants no plan of `live`, or None."""
    if state != "open":
        return f"pull request #{pr} is {state}; nothing dispatched"
    if draft:
        return f"pull request #{pr} moved to {live}, a draft; nothing dispatched"
    return None


def _changed(pr, live):
    """Why the pull request, read again after the wait, no longer wants `live` planned, or
    None."""
    now, draft, state = pr_head(_repo(), pr, run=run)
    if state == "open" and now != live:
        return (
            f"pull request #{pr} moved from {live} to {now} while this run waited; "
            "nothing dispatched"
        )
    return _stop(pr, live, draft, state)


def _dispatched(pr):
    try:
        r = other_live_plan_run(_repo(), pr, os.environ.get("GITHUB_RUN_ID", ""), run)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(str(exc)) from None
    if r is None:
        return None
    status = status_text(r.get("status"))
    return f"; its shipmate plan run {r['id']} is {status} and plans the live head"


def _replan(sha, pr):
    live, draft, state = pr_head(_repo(), pr, run=run)
    if live == sha:
        return
    if stop := _stop(pr, live, draft, state):
        print(stop, file=sys.stderr)
        return
    runs, evidence, polls = _autoplans(live), {}, REPLAN_WAIT // REPLAN_POLL
    reason = _unfinished(runs) or _dispatched(pr) or _succeeded(runs, evidence, polls == 0)
    for poll in range(polls):
        if reason:
            break
        sleep(REPLAN_POLL)
        runs = _autoplans(live)
        reason = _unfinished(runs) or _succeeded(runs, evidence, poll == polls - 1)
    if not reason and (changed := _changed(pr, live)):
        print(changed, file=sys.stderr)
        return
    reason = reason or _dispatched(pr)
    if reason:
        print(f"pull request #{pr} moved to {live}{reason}; nothing dispatched", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    print(live)


def _published_gate(sha):
    try:
        return newest_gate(sha)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(exit_text(exc))) from None
    except KeyError as exc:
        raise SystemExit(f"a statuses row of {sha} has no {exc}") from None


def _summary_completed(run_id, jobs):
    """The `completed_at` of run `run_id`'s one `plan / summary` job, `completed` `success`."""
    summaries = [j for j in jobs if j.get("name") == ag.CALLER_PREFIX + "summary"]
    shape = [(j.get("status"), j.get("conclusion")) for j in summaries]
    if shape != [("completed", "success")]:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(f"autoplan run {run_id} succeeded with summary jobs {shape}"))
    done = summaries[0].get("completed_at")
    if not isinstance(done, str) or not _CREATED_AT.fullmatch(done):
        raise SystemExit(one_line(f"autoplan run {run_id}'s summary completed at {done!r}"))
    return done


def _created_at(run_id):
    try:
        created = gh_json(f"repos/{_repo()}/actions/runs/{run_id}", run).get("created_at")
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(exit_text(exc))) from None
    except (ValueError, AttributeError) as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(f"run {run_id} cannot be read: {exc!r}")) from None
    if not isinstance(created, str) or not _CREATED_AT.fullmatch(created):
        raise SystemExit(one_line(f"run {run_id} was created at {created!r}"))
    return created


def _newest_plan_comment(pr):
    """The first four lines of the newest comment on `pr` by the shipmate App's bot user whose
    first line is `summary-comment.MARKER`, or None."""
    # Loaded here, not at import: no other command depends on summary-comment.
    marker = _load("summary-comment").MARKER
    login = f"{_require_slug()}[bot]"
    args = ["gh", "api", "--paginate", "--jq", _COMMENTS_JQ]
    args.append(f"repos/{_repo()}/issues/{pr}/comments?per_page=100")
    try:
        out = retried(lambda: run(args))
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise SystemExit(one_line(exit_text(exc))) from None
    newest = None
    for line in filter(str.strip, out.splitlines()):
        try:
            row = json.loads(line)
            lines = row["lines"]
            if row["login"] == login and lines[0] == marker:
                newest = lines
        except (ValueError, LookupError, TypeError) as exc:
            raise SystemExit(
                one_line(f"unparseable comment listing line {line!r}: {exc!r}")
            ) from None
    return newest


def _names(lines, sha, run_id):
    """Whether `lines`' fourth line ends with `summary-comment.provenance` of `sha` in run
    `run_id`."""
    server, repo = os.environ["GITHUB_SERVER_URL"], _repo()
    tail = (
        rf" at \[{sha[:7]}\]\({re.escape(f'{server}/{repo}/commit/{sha}')}\)"
        rf" in \[run #[0-9]+\]\({re.escape(current_run_url(run_id))}\)\Z"
    )
    return len(lines) > 3 and re.search(tail, lines[3]) is not None


def _published(sha, pr):
    """Print run `G` when every rule holds, else nothing:

    1. The newest App gate on `sha` (`newest_gate`) is `pending` or `success` and names
       run `G`, and is no hold (`is_hold`). A hold is a publish in progress or a plan that ended
       before its final gate, and only a plan clears it.
    2. `G` is not this run (`GITHUB_RUN_ID`): a re-run of a run that planned plans again.
    3. `G` is one of `sha`'s autoplan runs, listed `completed` with conclusion `success`.
    4. `G`'s jobs, every one at the listed run attempt, are `planned` (`apply-gate.planned`)
       and hold one `plan / summary` job `completed` `success`. A draft-skipped run is not
       planned, though its draft gate is `pending` too.
    5. The newest comment on `pr` by `<SHIPMATE_APP_SLUG>[bot]` whose first line is the plan
       comment's marker has a fourth line, the verdict, ending with `G`'s provenance of `sha`.
       The gate and the listing are commit-scoped and cannot tell two pull requests on one head
       apart; a later App plan comment (a dispatched plan) names another run.
    6. `G`'s `plan / summary` completed strictly after this run's `created_at`: this run was
       created while `G` had not published, the ready click racing the push run. Timestamps
       have whole seconds, so an equal pair cannot prove that order.

    `summary` writes `G`'s final gate, never a hold, last, after the comment and the apply
    checks, so rules 1-4 hold for a zero-cell plan and fail for a superseded summary, which writes
    no final gate. It reads in rule order and stops at the first rule that fails. Any other `plan /
    summary` shape under a planned, successful run, a summary without a `completed_at`, and
    every failed or malformed read exit 1 with one unannotated stderr line, as `replan` does."""
    gate = _published_gate(sha)
    if gate is None or gate[0] not in ("pending", "success") or is_hold(gate):
        return
    own, g = os.environ.get("GITHUB_RUN_ID", ""), gate[1]
    if not is_run_id(own):
        raise SystemExit(f"GITHUB_RUN_ID must be a decimal run id (got: {own!r})")
    if g == own:
        return
    listed = next((r for r in _autoplans(sha) if str(r["id"]) == g), None)
    if listed is None or (listed["status"], listed["conclusion"]) != ("completed", "success"):
        return
    jobs, attempts = _jobs(g)
    if attempts != [listed["run_attempt"]] or not _planned(g, jobs):
        return
    done = _summary_completed(g, jobs)
    lines = _newest_plan_comment(pr)
    if lines is None or not _names(lines, sha, g):
        return
    if done > _created_at(own):
        print(g)


#: command -> (argument count after the sha, handler)
_COMMANDS = {
    "newest": (0, _newest),
    "refuse-newer": (0, _refuse_newer),
    "checks": (1, _checks),
    "moved": (1, _moved),
    "replan": (1, _replan),
    "published": (1, _published),
    "post": (2, _post),
    "hold": (1, lambda sha, description: post(sha, "failure", description)),
    "publishing": (0, lambda sha: post(sha, "pending", PUBLISH_HOLD.format(run=_this_run()))),
    "end": (1, _end),
    "refresh": (0, refresh),
    "deploy": (2, lambda sha, detect, results: post(sha, *deploy_verdict(detect, results))),
}


def main(argv):
    cmd, *args = argv or [""]
    if cmd not in _COMMANDS or len(args) != _COMMANDS[cmd][0] + 1:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"::error::usage: gate-status {'|'.join(_COMMANDS)} <sha> ... (got {argv})"
        )
    sha = args[0]
    if cmd == "deploy" and not sha:
        raise SystemExit("::error::detect produced no head SHA; deploy cannot gate")
    # Unannotated for these: each caller annotates, as a warning where it degrades.
    validate_head_sha(
        sha, "head SHA", annotate=cmd not in ("checks", "moved", "replan", "published")
    )
    if cmd in ("moved", "replan", "published") and not is_pr_number(args[1]):
        raise SystemExit(f"::error::<pr> must be a pull request number (got: {args[1]!r})")
    _COMMANDS[cmd][1](*args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    run_annotated(main, sys.argv[1:])
